,"r":"ingredient/rice-wine","n":"Rice wine","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Rice wine is an alcoholic beverage fermented from rice, traditionally consumed in East Asia, Southeast Asia and South Asia, where rice is a quintessential staple crop. Rice wine is made by the fermentation of rice starch, during which microbes enzymatically convert polysaccharides to sugar and then to ethanol. The Chinese mijiu (most famous being huangjiu), Japanese sake, and Korean cheongju, dansul and takju are some of the most notable types of rice wine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-rice-wine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rice_wine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Cantonese cuisine","cuisine/cantonese-cuisine",[["Char siu","dish/char-siu"]]],["Taiwan cuisine","cuisine/taiwan-cuisine",[["Ló͘-bah-pn̄g","dish/lo-bah-png"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Dark soy sauce","ingredient/dark-soy-sauce"],["Fermented bean curd","ingredient/fermented-bean-curd"],["Five-spice powder","ingredient/five-spice-powder"],["Fortified wine","ingredient/fortified-wine"],["Hoisin sauce","ingredient/hoisin-sauce"],["Honey","ingredient/honey"],["Maltose","ingredient/maltose"],["Lurou","ingredient/lurou"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/18/%27Haj%27_rice_wine_made_from_fermented_rice.webm/500px--%27Haj%27_rice_wine_made_from_fermented_rice.webm.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Jyoti Chiring","lic":"CC BY 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:'Haj'_rice_wine_made_from_fermented_rice.webm"}},"dish/oysters-rockefeller":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/oysters-rockefeller","n":"Oysters Rockefeller","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Louisiana Creole cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Oysters Rockefeller is a dish consisting of oysters on the half-shell that have been topped with a rich sauce of butter, parsley and other green herbs, bread crumbs, and then baked or broiled. Lemon wedges are the typical garnish. Many contemporary adaptations use diced oysters instead of whole. Also, diced bacon often appears as a non-traditional topping in addition to or in place of the sauce."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-oysters-rockefeller"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oysters_Rockefeller"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Louisiana Creole cuisine","cuisine/louisiana-creole-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Oyster","ingredient/oyster"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Kenneth Temple: Cajun and Creole","ref":"site/kennethtemple-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"kennethtemple.com","note":"Cajun and Creole recipes from a New Orleans-based cook and video creator.","url":"https://kennethtemple.com/recipes/cajun-creole/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Louisiana Creole cuisine","links":[["Brennan's","dish/brennan-s"],["Eggs Sardou","dish/eggs-sardou"],["New Orleans hot sausage","dish/new-orleans-hot-sausage"],["Oysters Bienville","dish/oysters-bienville"],["Red beans and rice","dish/red-beans-and-rice"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b9/Oysters_Rockefeller_at_Galatoires.jpg/500px-Oysters_Rockefeller_at_Galatoires.jpg","w":500,"h":343,"by":"David Veksler","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oysters_Rockefeller_at_Galatoires.jpg"}},"dish/papeda-food":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/papeda-food","n":"Papeda (food)","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Indonesian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Papeda, or bubur sagu, is a type of congee made from sago starch. It is a staple food of the people indigenous to Eastern Indonesia, namely parts of Sulawesi, the Maluku Islands and  coastal Papua. It is also widespread in Papua New Guinea and serves as the counterpart to central and western Indonesian cuisines that favour rice as their staple food. Papeda is of Ambon Malay origin and attested in Vocabularium ofte Woort-boeck (1623) by Dutch clergymen Caspar Wiltens and Sebastiaan Danckaerts."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-papeda-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papeda_(food)"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Sago","ingredient/sago"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","ref":"site/dailycookingquest-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/db/Hidangan_papeda.jpg/500px-Hidangan_papeda.jpg","w":500,"h":454,"by":"Pungkas nurrohman","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hidangan_papeda.jpg"}},"dish/pirozhki":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pirozhki","n":"Pirozhki","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Pirozhki is the Russian name for baked or fried yeast-leavened boat-shaped buns with a variety of fillings in Russian and Eastern European cuisine in general. Pirozhki are a popular street food and comfort food. They are especially popular in countries with large ethnic Russian communities, and may also be found in other parts of the world."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pirozhki"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pirozhki"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Armenian cuisine","cuisine/armenian-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"The Armenian Kitchen","ref":"site/thearmeniankitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"thearmeniankitchen.com","note":"Armenian-American blog of family recipes and reviews of Armenian cookbooks.","url":"https://www.thearmeniankitchen.com/classic-armenian-recipes-another/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"Olga's Flavor Factory","ref":"site/olgasflavorfactory-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"olgasflavorfactory.com","note":"Russian-born home cook Olga's blog, with a dedicated Russian recipes category.","url":"https://www.olgasflavorfactory.com/category/russianrecipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Armenian cuisine","links":[["Alani (dried fruit)","dish/alani-dried-fruit"],["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Bagharj","dish/bagharj"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["İmam bayıldı","dish/imam-bay-ld"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khorovats","dish/khorovats"],["Lavash","dish/lavash"],["Matnakash","dish/matnakash"],["Mikado cake","dish/mikado-cake"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Spas (soup)","dish/spas-soup"],["Tjvjik (food)","dish/tjvjik-food"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/df/%D0%9F%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B6%D0%BA%D0%B8_03.jpg/500px-%D0%9F%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B6%D0%BA%D0%B8_03.jpg","w":500,"h":502,"by":"Pannet","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%9F%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B6%D0%BA%D0%B8_03.jpg"}},"ingredient/pangium":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/pangium","n":"Pangium","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Pangium is a genus in the family Achariaceae containing the sole species Pangium edule, a tall tree native to the mangrove swamps of Southeast Asia (from the Philippines, Malaysia, Indonesia to Papua New Guinea, and extending eastward to the Vanuatu and Micronesia).  It produces a large poisonous fruit (the \"football fruit\" or pangi) which can be made edible by fermentation. It is dioecious, with male and female flowers produced on separate individuals."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-pangium"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pangium"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Rawon","dish/rawon"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Bay leaf","ingredient/bay-leaf"],["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Celery","ingredient/celery"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Keluak","ingredient/keluak"],["Mung bean sprout","ingredient/mung-bean-sprout"],["Palm oil","ingredient/palm-oil"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Scallion","ingredient/scallion"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/8b/Pangium_edule_Blanco2.391.jpg/500px-Pangium_edule_Blanco2.391.jpg","w":500,"h":721,"by":"Francisco Manuel Blanco (O.S.A.)","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pangium_edule_Blanco2.391.jpg"}},"ingredient/dl-lactic-acid":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/dl-lactic-acid","n":"DL-lactic acid","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 7 cuisines","intro":[["Lactic acid is an organic acid found in milk with the molecular formula C3H6O3. In its solid state, it is white and in its liquid state is miscible with water. When dissolved, it forms a colorless solution. Production includes both artificial synthesis and natural sources. Lactic acid is an alpha-hydroxy acid (AHA) due to the presence of a hydroxyl group adjacent to the carboxyl group. It is a synthetic intermediate in many organic synthesis industries and in various biochemical industries. The conjugate base of lactic acid is called lactate (or the lactate anion). The name of the derived acyl group is lactoyl."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-dl-lactic-acid"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lactic_acid"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":7,"groups":[["Bulgarian cuisine","cuisine/bulgarian-cuisine",[["Sauerkraut","dish/sauerkraut"]]],["Czech cuisine","cuisine/czech-cuisine",[["Sauerkraut","dish/sauerkraut"]]],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine",[["Sauerkraut","dish/sauerkraut"]]],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine",[["Sauerkraut","dish/sauerkraut"]]],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-